 -- Kernora Developers <developers@kernora.invalid>  Mon, 12 Oct 2026 12:00:00 -0700

kernora-desktop (1.4.23) UNRELEASED; urgency=medium

  * Graphics drivers install themselves. kernora-drivers looks for an NVIDIA
    display controller in /sys/bus/pci; without one it does nothing and
    downloads nothing. GeForce GTX 16 / RTX 20 and newer (PCI device
    0x1e00 and up, RTX 50 included) get nvidia-open from NVIDIA's signed
    Debian 13 repository (key 02182E60104FCDC26EAE1B8597A5D4CB8793F200,
    pinned so that only NVIDIA's driver packages come from it); GTX 900 and
    10 get Debian's nvidia-driver from contrib and non-free; older cards stay
    on nouveau. A failed install is rolled back.
  * kernora-drivers.timer runs the check 90 seconds after it starts (at boot
    or right after the package is installed) and every six
    hours, without delaying the login screen. The live session and systems
    with Secure Boot on are left alone; /etc/kernora/drivers.conf turns the
    automatic install off.
  * The package enables and starts only the timer: starting the oneshot
    service from postinst would run apt-get inside the dpkg transaction and
    wait for its own lock.
  * The installer installs the driver into the new system when it is
    online (shellprocess@drivers after the update step).
  * kernora-drivers@install and @remove serve Settings through pkexec
    (org.kernora.drivers).

